steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787498711
      nanos: 614735771
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Build a production-quality web application called Resume Generation Pipeline.\nThe application is an automated AI-powered resume generation and management system. It should take candidate information from Google Sheets, optionally take a Job Description, use Gemini to intelligently generate and tailor resume content, generate a professional LaTeX resume, compile it into a PDF, upload the PDF to Google Drive, and write the generated resume URL and processing status back to Google Sheets.\nThe application must have a professional dark dashboard UI and should feel like an internal enterprise automation tool rather than a simple form.\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not create a fake/static UI.\nEvery major button must perform a real operation where technically possible.\nClearly separate frontend logic, API logic, authentication, AI generation, document generation, and storage.\nNever expose private API keys, OAuth client secrets, refresh tokens, or service credentials in frontend source code.\nUse environment variables/secrets or secure server-side functionality wherever possible.\nIf a required external service cannot be accessed directly from the browser, create a clean backend/API abstraction and provide a clearly marked demo/mock implementation rather than pretending it works.\nDo not hardcode credentials.\nBuild the application so it can later be deployed outside AI Studio with minimal restructuring.\n==================================================\nAPPLICATION NAME\nName:\nResume Generation Pipeline\nVersion:\nv1.0.0\nSubtitle:\nAutomated AI resume compilation using Gemini and Google Workspace integrations.\nThe main dashboard should contain:\nApplication title\nVersion number\nShort description\nRUN PIPELINE button\nPipeline progress indicator\nConfiguration Settings\nLive Runtime Console\nWorksheet Row Tracker\nAuthentication / Credentials section\nGenerated Resume History\n==================================================\n2. CORE PIPELINE\nThe application must implement this pipeline:\nFETCH\n\342\206\223\nSHEET\n\342\206\223\nGEMINI\n\342\206\223\nLATEX\n\342\206\223\nCOMPILE\n\342\206\223\nDRIVE\n\342\206\223\nSHEETS COMMIT\nEach stage should have its own visual status:\nPending\nRunning\nSuccess\nFailed\nSkipped\nShow the stages horizontally near the top of the dashboard.\nExample:\n01 FETCH\n02 SHEET\n03 GEMINI\n04 LATEX\n05 COMPILE\n06 DRIVE\n07 SHEETS COMMIT\nWhen RUN PIPELINE is pressed, animate the stages as the pipeline executes.\n==================================================\n3. GOOGLE SHEETS INTEGRATION\nAllow the user to connect a Google account using OAuth 2.0.\nRequired Google permissions should be limited to the minimum necessary scopes.\nThe application should be capable of:\nSelecting a Google Spreadsheet.\nSelecting a worksheet/tab.\nReading candidate rows.\nIdentifying the candidate.\nReading candidate information.\nProcessing one candidate.\nProcessing multiple candidates.\nWriting processing status back to the spreadsheet.\nWriting the generated Google Drive URL back to the spreadsheet.\nCreate a configuration interface containing:\nGoogle Spreadsheet ID\nWorksheet Name\nCandidate Row\nCandidate ID Column\nStatus Column\nResume URL Column\nJob Description Column\nProvide a \"Test Connection\" button.\nDo not assume a particular spreadsheet ID.\nAllow the user to enter or select the spreadsheet dynamically.\n==================================================\n4. GOOGLE SHEETS DATA MODEL\nSupport a spreadsheet structure similar to:\nCandidate ID\nName\nEmail\nPhone\nLocation\nLinkedIn\nGitHub\nPortfolio\nSummary\nEducation\nSkills\nExperience\nProjects\nCertifications\nAchievements\nJob Description\nStatus\nResume URL\nGenerated At\nError\nThe application should not require every field to exist.\nIt should gracefully handle missing fields.\nAllow column mapping so the user can map spreadsheet columns to resume fields.\nCreate a visual column-mapping interface.\nExample:\nName \342\206\222 \"Candidate Name\"\nEmail \342\206\222 \"Email Address\"\nSkills \342\206\222 \"Technical Skills\"\nExperience \342\206\222 \"Work Experience\"\nProjects \342\206\222 \"Projects\"\nJob Description \342\206\222 \"Target JD\"\n==================================================\n5. JOB DESCRIPTION PROCESSING\nAllow the user to provide a Job Description in three ways:\nRead it from a Google Sheets column.\nPaste it into a text area.\nUpload/import a text document if supported.\nThe system should analyze the Job Description and extract:\nRequired skills\nPreferred skills\nTechnologies\nResponsibilities\nRole title\nSeniority\nDomain\nImportant keywords\nATS-relevant terminology\nShow the extracted information before generating the resume.\n==================================================\n6. GEMINI AI GENERATION\nUse the currently available Gemini model configured through the application\'s secure environment.\nDo not hardcode a model unnecessarily.\nCreate a dedicated AI generation service.\nThe AI must generate resume content based ONLY on the candidate\'s supplied information.\nCRITICAL ANTI-HALLUCINATION RULE:\nNever invent:\ncompanies\njob titles\ndegrees\ndates\ncertifications\ntechnologies\nachievements\nmetrics\nresponsibilities\nprojects\nawards\nIf information is missing, do not fabricate it.\nThe AI may rewrite, restructure, condense, improve grammar, and optimize wording, but factual claims must remain grounded in the source candidate data.\n==================================================\n7. RESUME GENERATION RULES\nCreate configurable strict formatting rules.\nDefault rules:\nPAGE FORMAT:\nPrefer 1 page for candidates with limited experience.\nUse 2 pages when the candidate has sufficient relevant content.\nAvoid large empty areas.\nNever artificially invent information merely to fill space.\nWORK EXPERIENCE:\nUp to 5 bullets per experience entry.\nBullets should contain meaningful technical/professional information.\nPrefer approximately 1\342\200\2233 lines per bullet depending on layout.\nUse strong action verbs.\nPreserve factual accuracy.\nQuantify results only when the source data contains valid metrics.\nPROJECTS:\nInclude up to 2\342\200\2234 relevant projects.\nPrefer 2\342\200\2233 bullets per project depending on available information.\nPrioritize projects relevant to the target Job Description.\nSKILLS:\nOrganize technical skills into sensible categories.\nPrioritize skills relevant to the Job Description.\nNever add a skill merely because it appears in the Job Description unless the candidate data confirms it.\nSUMMARY:\nGenerate a concise professional summary.\nTailor it toward the target role.\nDo not make unsupported claims.\nATS OPTIMIZATION:\nNaturally incorporate relevant keywords from the Job Description when supported by candidate information.\nDo not keyword-stuff.\nDo not manipulate the resume with hidden text or other ATS tricks.\n==================================================\n8. GEMINI OUTPUT FORMAT\nDo NOT ask Gemini to directly generate an arbitrary PDF.\nHave Gemini return structured JSON representing the resume.\nExample structure:\n{\n\"candidate\": {},\n\"summary\": \"\",\n\"skills\": {},\n\"experience\": [],\n\"projects\": [],\n\"education\": [],\n\"certifications\": [],\n\"achievements\": [],\n\"ats_keywords_used\": [],\n\"warnings\": []\n}\nValidate the JSON against a schema before continuing.\nIf Gemini returns invalid JSON:\nAttempt structured repair.\nRetry generation if necessary.\nDisplay an error in the runtime console if validation still fails.\nDo not pass unvalidated AI output directly into the LaTeX compiler.\n==================================================\n9. LATEX GENERATION\nCreate a dedicated LaTeX generation layer.\nConvert the validated resume JSON into a professional LaTeX document.\nUse a clean ATS-friendly resume layout.\nThe LaTeX template should be deterministic.\nGemini should generate the CONTENT.\nThe application should generate the FINAL LATEX STRUCTURE.\nDo not allow Gemini to freely change the LaTeX template.\nCreate a reusable template system.\nSupport:\n1-page resume\n2-page resume\nconsistent typography\nconsistent margins\nsection headings\nbullet lists\nhyperlinks\neducation\nexperience\nprojects\nskills\ncertifications\nachievements\nEscape LaTeX special characters correctly.\nHandle characters such as:\n&\n%\n$\n_\n{\n}\nand other LaTeX-sensitive characters.\n==================================================\n10. PDF COMPILATION\nCompile the generated .tex file into a PDF.\nBecause browser JavaScript cannot reliably perform arbitrary server-side LaTeX compilation, create a dedicated compilation abstraction.\nArchitecture:\nFrontend\n\342\206\223\nResume API\n\342\206\223\nLaTeX compiler\n\342\206\223\nPDF\nThe compiler implementation should be replaceable.\nSupport a secure server-side compilation service or deployment-compatible compilation endpoint.\nDo NOT execute arbitrary LaTeX supplied directly by unauthenticated users.\nRestrict compilation to generated template content.\nCapture:\ncompilation status\ncompiler output\nerrors\nwarnings\ngenerated PDF\nIf compilation fails, show the actual useful error in the Runtime Console.\n==================================================\n11. GOOGLE DRIVE\nAfter successful PDF compilation:\nCreate or locate a configured Drive folder.\nUpload the generated PDF.\nUse a predictable filename.\nExample:\nCandidateName_Resume_2026.pdf\nAllow the user to configure:\nDrive Folder ID\nOptionally create a candidate-specific folder.\nStore:\nFile ID\nFile Name\nDrive URL\nGenerated timestamp\nDo not automatically make files publicly accessible unless the user explicitly enables this.\nProvide configurable sharing behavior:\nPRIVATE\nDOMAIN\nLINK_ACCESS\nExplain the security implications of link sharing.\n==================================================\n12. GOOGLE SHEETS COMMIT\nAfter successful Drive upload:\nUpdate the candidate row with:\nStatus = COMPLETED\nResume URL = Drive URL\nGenerated At = timestamp\nFile ID = Drive file ID\nIf anything fails:\nStatus = FAILED\nError = useful error message\nThe update must happen only after the previous pipeline stages succeed.\n==================================================\n13. LIVE RUNTIME CONSOLE\nCreate a live console panel.\nExample:\n[14:21:01] Pipeline started\n[14:21:02] Fetching spreadsheet\n[14:21:03] Candidate identified: John Doe\n[14:21:04] Reading candidate data\n[14:21:05] Analyzing Job Description\n[14:21:08] Gemini generation started\n[14:21:14] Resume JSON validated\n[14:21:15] Generating LaTeX\n[14:21:16] Compiling PDF\n[14:21:20] PDF compilation successful\n[14:21:21] Uploading to Google Drive\n[14:21:23] Drive upload successful\n[14:21:24] Updating Google Sheet\n[14:21:25] PIPELINE COMPLETED\nEach log entry should have:\ntimestamp\nstage\nseverity\nmessage\nSeverity:\nINFO\nSUCCESS\nWARNING\nERROR\nAllow:\nClear console\nCopy logs\nDownload logs if practical\n==================================================\n14. WORKSHEET ROW TRACKER\nCreate a table showing candidate processing status.\nColumns:\nRow\nCandidate\nTarget Role\nStatus\nGemini\nLaTeX\nPDF\nDrive\nSheets\nGenerated At\nResume\nUse status indicators.\nExample:\nJohn Doe | COMPLETED | \342\234\223 | \342\234\223 | \342\234\223 | \342\234\223 | \342\234\223\nAllow clicking a row to inspect the full pipeline execution.\n==================================================\n15. BULK PROCESSING\nSupport:\nPROCESS ONE\nPROCESS SELECTED\nPROCESS ALL\nFor bulk processing:\nProcess candidates sequentially by default.\nAvoid excessive API concurrency.\nRespect Gemini and Google API rate limits.\nDisplay progress.\nExample:\nProcessing 12 / 50\nDo not lose successful results if one candidate fails.\nA failed candidate should not stop the entire batch unless the user explicitly selects \"Stop on error.\"\n==================================================\n16. RETRY SYSTEM\nEvery pipeline stage should support retry where appropriate.\nFor example:\nRetry Gemini\nRetry LaTeX\nRetry PDF compilation\nRetry Drive upload\nRetry Sheets commit\nDo not regenerate the entire resume unnecessarily if only Drive upload failed.\nPersist enough pipeline state to resume from the failed stage.\n==================================================\n17. CONFIGURATION SETTINGS\nCreate a Settings panel containing:\nGOOGLE WORKSPACE\nGoogle account\nSpreadsheet\nWorksheet\nDrive folder\nColumn mapping\nAI\nGemini model\nGeneration temperature if supported\nMaximum retry count\nStrict factual mode\nRESUME\nTemplate\nPage target\nMaximum experience bullets\nMaximum projects\nFont\nMargins\nPIPELINE\nSequential/bulk mode\nStop on error\nRetry count\nLogging level\nSECURITY\nDrive sharing mode\nSession timeout\nCredential status\n==================================================\n18. CREDENTIALS UI\nCreate a secure credentials section.\nShow:\nGoogle Workspace OAuth\nConnected / Not Connected\nGemini\nConfigured / Not Configured\nNever display complete secrets.\nFor example:\nGemini API Key\n\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\nDo not store sensitive credentials in localStorage unless absolutely necessary.\nPrefer secure environment variables / platform secrets / OAuth token storage mechanisms supported by the deployment environment.\nIf the environment cannot securely store credentials, clearly explain what must be configured during deployment.\n==================================================\n19. ERROR HANDLING\nHandle:\nGoogle OAuth failure\nGoogle Sheets permission failure\nSpreadsheet not found\nWorksheet not found\nInvalid row\nMissing candidate information\nGemini API failure\nGemini rate limit\nInvalid Gemini response\nSchema validation failure\nLaTeX generation failure\nLaTeX compilation failure\nPDF generation failure\nDrive permission failure\nDrive upload failure\nSheets update failure\nNetwork failure\nEvery error should identify:\nStage\nCause\nSuggested action\nExample:\nDRIVE ERROR\nUnable to upload resume.\nPossible cause:\nThe authenticated Google account does not have permission to write to the selected Drive folder.\nAction:\nReconnect Google Workspace or select another Drive folder.\n==================================================\n20. UI DESIGN\nDesign a modern dark enterprise dashboard.\nVisual style:\ndark background\ncharcoal panels\nsubtle borders\nhigh contrast text\nrestrained accent color\nclean typography\ncompact information density\nrounded cards\nprofessional technical dashboard aesthetic\nTop section:\nResume Generation Pipeline\nv1.0.0\n[ RUN PIPELINE ]\nBelow:\nFETCH \342\206\222 SHEET \342\206\222 GEMINI \342\206\222 LATEX \342\206\222 COMPILE \342\206\222 DRIVE \342\206\222 COMMIT\nThen tabs:\nCONFIGURATION SETTINGS\nLIVE RUNTIME CONSOLE\nWORKSHEET ROW TRACKER\nGENERATED RESUMES\nDo not make the UI excessively flashy.\nPrioritize usability.\nThe application must be responsive for desktop and tablet.\n==================================================\n21. GENERATED RESUME PREVIEW\nAfter generating a resume, display:\nCandidate name\nTarget role\nATS keyword analysis\nGeneration status\nPDF preview if technically supported\nDownload PDF\nOpen Drive\nRegenerate\nView LaTeX\nView generation warnings\nAlso show:\nAI-generated content warnings\nFor example:\n\"3 fields were missing from the source data.\"\n==================================================\n22. SECURITY ARCHITECTURE\nTreat security as a first-class requirement.\nNever:\nhardcode Google OAuth secrets\nhardcode Gemini API keys\nexpose refresh tokens\nexpose service account private keys\nput credentials into client-side source\nmake Drive files public automatically\ntrust raw AI-generated LaTeX\nexecute arbitrary user-provided code\nUse:\nOAuth 2.0\nleast-privilege Google scopes\nenvironment secrets\ninput validation\noutput schema validation\nsanitized LaTeX\nauthenticated backend endpoints\nrate limiting where appropriate\n==================================================\n23. CODE ARCHITECTURE\nSeparate the application into logical modules.\nSuggested architecture:\n/src\n/components\n/pages\n/services\ngeminiService\nsheetsService\ndriveService\noauthService\nlatexService\npipelineService\n/types\n/schemas\n/utils\n/hooks\nBackend/API layer:\n/server\n/routes\n/services\n/auth\n/latex\n/google\n/gemini\nUse TypeScript if supported.\nCreate strong types for:\nCandidate\nJobDescription\nResume\nExperience\nProject\nPipelineState\nPipelineLog\nGoogleSheetConfig\nDriveConfig\nUse schema validation for external/API data.\n==================================================\n24. PIPELINE STATE MACHINE\nRepresent pipeline execution explicitly.\nExample:\nIDLE\n\342\206\223\nFETCHING\n\342\206\223\nSHEET_LOADED\n\342\206\223\nAI_GENERATING\n\342\206\223\nAI_VALIDATED\n\342\206\223\nLATEX_GENERATED\n\342\206\223\nPDF_COMPILING\n\342\206\223\nPDF_READY\n\342\206\223\nDRIVE_UPLOADING\n\342\206\223\nDRIVE_COMPLETE\n\342\206\223\nSHEETS_COMMITTING\n\342\206\223\nCOMPLETED\nAny stage may transition to:\nFAILED\nand expose:\nRetry\n==================================================\n25. DEMO MODE\nThe application must include a DEMO MODE so the UI can be tested without Google credentials.\nDemo mode should use realistic sample data.\nExample candidate:\nAlex Morgan\nB.Tech Computer Science\nPython\nJava\nSQL\nAWS\nReact\nMachine Learning\nCreate a sample Job Description.\nWhen Demo Mode is enabled:\nDo not call real Google APIs.\nDo not upload anything.\nSimulate pipeline stages.\nGenerate realistic logs.\nDemonstrate the complete UI.\nClearly label:\nDEMO MODE\nNever confuse simulated results with real results.\n==================================================\n26. IMPORTANT IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENT\nBefore implementing complicated features, inspect what Google AI Studio\'s current environment actually supports.\nDo not invent unavailable APIs or capabilities.\nIf something cannot run directly inside the AI Studio client:\nKeep the frontend interface.\nCreate a clean service abstraction.\nExplain the required backend/deployment component.\nProvide a working mock/demo implementation.\nKeep the production implementation replaceable.\nThe final application should therefore be architected so that:\nDEMO:\nBrowser\n\342\206\223\nMock Services\nPRODUCTION:\nBrowser\n\342\206\223\nSecure Backend/API\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Gemini\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Google OAuth\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Google Sheets API\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Google Drive API\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 LaTeX Compiler\n==================================================\n27. BUILD ORDER\nImplement in this order:\nPHASE 1\nCreate the complete dashboard UI and navigation.\nPHASE 2\nCreate the pipeline state machine and runtime console.\nPHASE 3\nCreate Demo Mode and make the entire pipeline work using mock services.\nPHASE 4\nImplement Gemini structured resume generation.\nPHASE 5\nImplement Google OAuth.\nPHASE 6\nImplement Google Sheets reading and writing.\nPHASE 7\nImplement LaTeX generation.\nPHASE 8\nImplement secure PDF compilation.\nPHASE 9\nImplement Google Drive upload.\nPHASE 10\nImplement Sheets commit.\nPHASE 11\nImplement bulk processing and retry/resume functionality.\nPHASE 12\nPerform security and error-handling review.\nDo not move to the next phase while the previous phase has broken functionality.\n==================================================\n28. FINAL SUCCESS CRITERIA\nThe finished application should allow this workflow:\nUser opens Resume Generation Pipeline.\nUser connects Google account.\nUser selects Google Spreadsheet.\nUser selects worksheet.\nUser maps columns.\nUser selects candidate.\nUser provides or selects Job Description.\nUser presses RUN PIPELINE.\nApplication fetches candidate data.\nApplication analyzes the Job Description.\nGemini generates structured resume content.\nApplication validates the Gemini response.\nApplication generates deterministic LaTeX.\nLaTeX is securely compiled to PDF.\nPDF is uploaded to Google Drive.\nDrive URL is obtained.\nGoogle Sheet is updated.\nUser sees COMPLETED.\nUser can preview/download/open the generated resume.\nAll stages and logs remain visible in the runtime console.\nBuild this as a real application, not merely a visual prototype.\nStart by creating the complete application architecture and functional Demo Mode, then progressively connect the real Google/Gemini services.\n"
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Add Gemini intelligence\"\nicon: \"spark\"\n---\n\nYou MUST add Gemini to the app where relevant to complete all sorts of tasks - analyze content, make edits, and more. These are suggested ideas. Use gemini-3.1-pro-preview for complex tasks, gemini-3.5-flash for general tasks, and gemini-3.1-flash-lite for tasks that should happen fast."
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Transcribe audio\"\nicon: \"speech_to_text\"\n---\n\nAdd a feature where users can input audio with their microphone and the app with transcribe it. You MUST add audio transcription to the app using model gemini-3.5-flash"
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      text: "Build a production-quality web application called Resume Generation Pipeline.\nThe application is an automated AI-powered resume generation and management system. It should take candidate information from Google Sheets, optionally take a Job Description, use Gemini to intelligently generate and tailor resume content, generate a professional LaTeX resume, compile it into a PDF, upload the PDF to Google Drive, and write the generated resume URL and processing status back to Google Sheets.\nThe application must have a professional dark dashboard UI and should feel like an internal enterprise automation tool rather than a simple form.\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not create a fake/static UI.\nEvery major button must perform a real operation where technically possible.\nClearly separate frontend logic, API logic, authentication, AI generation, document generation, and storage.\nNever expose private API keys, OAuth client secrets, refresh tokens, or service credentials in frontend source code.\nUse environment variables/secrets or secure server-side functionality wherever possible.\nIf a required external service cannot be accessed directly from the browser, create a clean backend/API abstraction and provide a clearly marked demo/mock implementation rather than pretending it works.\nDo not hardcode credentials.\nBuild the application so it can later be deployed outside AI Studio with minimal restructuring.\n==================================================\nAPPLICATION NAME\nName:\nResume Generation Pipeline\nVersion:\nv1.0.0\nSubtitle:\nAutomated AI resume compilation using Gemini and Google Workspace integrations.\nThe main dashboard should contain:\nApplication title\nVersion number\nShort description\nRUN PIPELINE button\nPipeline progress indicator\nConfiguration Settings\nLive Runtime Console\nWorksheet Row Tracker\nAuthentication / Credentials section\nGenerated Resume History\n==================================================\n2. CORE PIPELINE\nThe application must implement this pipeline:\nFETCH\n\342\206\223\nSHEET\n\342\206\223\nGEMINI\n\342\206\223\nLATEX\n\342\206\223\nCOMPILE\n\342\206\223\nDRIVE\n\342\206\223\nSHEETS COMMIT\nEach stage should have its own visual status:\nPending\nRunning\nSuccess\nFailed\nSkipped\nShow the stages horizontally near the top of the dashboard.\nExample:\n01 FETCH\n02 SHEET\n03 GEMINI\n04 LATEX\n05 COMPILE\n06 DRIVE\n07 SHEETS COMMIT\nWhen RUN PIPELINE is pressed, animate the stages as the pipeline executes.\n==================================================\n3. GOOGLE SHEETS INTEGRATION\nAllow the user to connect a Google account using OAuth 2.0.\nRequired Google permissions should be limited to the minimum necessary scopes.\nThe application should be capable of:\nSelecting a Google Spreadsheet.\nSelecting a worksheet/tab.\nReading candidate rows.\nIdentifying the candidate.\nReading candidate information.\nProcessing one candidate.\nProcessing multiple candidates.\nWriting processing status back to the spreadsheet.\nWriting the generated Google Drive URL back to the spreadsheet.\nCreate a configuration interface containing:\nGoogle Spreadsheet ID\nWorksheet Name\nCandidate Row\nCandidate ID Column\nStatus Column\nResume URL Column\nJob Description Column\nProvide a \"Test Connection\" button.\nDo not assume a particular spreadsheet ID.\nAllow the user to enter or select the spreadsheet dynamically.\n==================================================\n4. GOOGLE SHEETS DATA MODEL\nSupport a spreadsheet structure similar to:\nCandidate ID\nName\nEmail\nPhone\nLocation\nLinkedIn\nGitHub\nPortfolio\nSummary\nEducation\nSkills\nExperience\nProjects\nCertifications\nAchievements\nJob Description\nStatus\nResume URL\nGenerated At\nError\nThe application should not require every field to exist.\nIt should gracefully handle missing fields.\nAllow column mapping so the user can map spreadsheet columns to resume fields.\nCreate a visual column-mapping interface.\nExample:\nName \342\206\222 \"Candidate Name\"\nEmail \342\206\222 \"Email Address\"\nSkills \342\206\222 \"Technical Skills\"\nExperience \342\206\222 \"Work Experience\"\nProjects \342\206\222 \"Projects\"\nJob Description \342\206\222 \"Target JD\"\n==================================================\n5. JOB DESCRIPTION PROCESSING\nAllow the user to provide a Job Description in three ways:\nRead it from a Google Sheets column.\nPaste it into a text area.\nUpload/import a text document if supported.\nThe system should analyze the Job Description and extract:\nRequired skills\nPreferred skills\nTechnologies\nResponsibilities\nRole title\nSeniority\nDomain\nImportant keywords\nATS-relevant terminology\nShow the extracted information before generating the resume.\n==================================================\n6. GEMINI AI GENERATION\nUse the currently available Gemini model configured through the application\'s secure environment.\nDo not hardcode a model unnecessarily.\nCreate a dedicated AI generation service.\nThe AI must generate resume content based ONLY on the candidate\'s supplied information.\nCRITICAL ANTI-HALLUCINATION RULE:\nNever invent:\ncompanies\njob titles\ndegrees\ndates\ncertifications\ntechnologies\nachievements\nmetrics\nresponsibilities\nprojects\nawards\nIf information is missing, do not fabricate it.\nThe AI may rewrite, restructure, condense, improve grammar, and optimize wording, but factual claims must remain grounded in the source candidate data.\n==================================================\n7. RESUME GENERATION RULES\nCreate configurable strict formatting rules.\nDefault rules:\nPAGE FORMAT:\nPrefer 1 page for candidates with limited experience.\nUse 2 pages when the candidate has sufficient relevant content.\nAvoid large empty areas.\nNever artificially invent information merely to fill space.\nWORK EXPERIENCE:\nUp to 5 bullets per experience entry.\nBullets should contain meaningful technical/professional information.\nPrefer approximately 1\342\200\2233 lines per bullet depending on layout.\nUse strong action verbs.\nPreserve factual accuracy.\nQuantify results only when the source data contains valid metrics.\nPROJECTS:\nInclude up to 2\342\200\2234 relevant projects.\nPrefer 2\342\200\2233 bullets per project depending on available information.\nPrioritize projects relevant to the target Job Description.\nSKILLS:\nOrganize technical skills into sensible categories.\nPrioritize skills relevant to the Job Description.\nNever add a skill merely because it appears in the Job Description unless the candidate data confirms it.\nSUMMARY:\nGenerate a concise professional summary.\nTailor it toward the target role.\nDo not make unsupported claims.\nATS OPTIMIZATION:\nNaturally incorporate relevant keywords from the Job Description when supported by candidate information.\nDo not keyword-stuff.\nDo not manipulate the resume with hidden text or other ATS tricks.\n==================================================\n8. GEMINI OUTPUT FORMAT\nDo NOT ask Gemini to directly generate an arbitrary PDF.\nHave Gemini return structured JSON representing the resume.\nExample structure:\n{\n\"candidate\": {},\n\"summary\": \"\",\n\"skills\": {},\n\"experience\": [],\n\"projects\": [],\n\"education\": [],\n\"certifications\": [],\n\"achievements\": [],\n\"ats_keywords_used\": [],\n\"warnings\": []\n}\nValidate the JSON against a schema before continuing.\nIf Gemini returns invalid JSON:\nAttempt structured repair.\nRetry generation if necessary.\nDisplay an error in the runtime console if validation still fails.\nDo not pass unvalidated AI output directly into the LaTeX compiler.\n==================================================\n9. LATEX GENERATION\nCreate a dedicated LaTeX generation layer.\nConvert the validated resume JSON into a professional LaTeX document.\nUse a clean ATS-friendly resume layout.\nThe LaTeX template should be deterministic.\nGemini should generate the CONTENT.\nThe application should generate the FINAL LATEX STRUCTURE.\nDo not allow Gemini to freely change the LaTeX template.\nCreate a reusable template system.\nSupport:\n1-page resume\n2-page resume\nconsistent typography\nconsistent margins\nsection headings\nbullet lists\nhyperlinks\neducation\nexperience\nprojects\nskills\ncertifications\nachievements\nEscape LaTeX special characters correctly.\nHandle characters such as:\n&\n%\n$\n_\n{\n}\nand other LaTeX-sensitive characters.\n==================================================\n10. PDF COMPILATION\nCompile the generated .tex file into a PDF.\nBecause browser JavaScript cannot reliably perform arbitrary server-side LaTeX compilation, create a dedicated compilation abstraction.\nArchitecture:\nFrontend\n\342\206\223\nResume API\n\342\206\223\nLaTeX compiler\n\342\206\223\nPDF\nThe compiler implementation should be replaceable.\nSupport a secure server-side compilation service or deployment-compatible compilation endpoint.\nDo NOT execute arbitrary LaTeX supplied directly by unauthenticated users.\nRestrict compilation to generated template content.\nCapture:\ncompilation status\ncompiler output\nerrors\nwarnings\ngenerated PDF\nIf compilation fails, show the actual useful error in the Runtime Console.\n==================================================\n11. GOOGLE DRIVE\nAfter successful PDF compilation:\nCreate or locate a configured Drive folder.\nUpload the generated PDF.\nUse a predictable filename.\nExample:\nCandidateName_Resume_2026.pdf\nAllow the user to configure:\nDrive Folder ID\nOptionally create a candidate-specific folder.\nStore:\nFile ID\nFile Name\nDrive URL\nGenerated timestamp\nDo not automatically make files publicly accessible unless the user explicitly enables this.\nProvide configurable sharing behavior:\nPRIVATE\nDOMAIN\nLINK_ACCESS\nExplain the security implications of link sharing.\n==================================================\n12. GOOGLE SHEETS COMMIT\nAfter successful Drive upload:\nUpdate the candidate row with:\nStatus = COMPLETED\nResume URL = Drive URL\nGenerated At = timestamp\nFile ID = Drive file ID\nIf anything fails:\nStatus = FAILED\nError = useful error message\nThe update must happen only after the previous pipeline stages succeed.\n==================================================\n13. LIVE RUNTIME CONSOLE\nCreate a live console panel.\nExample:\n[14:21:01] Pipeline started\n[14:21:02] Fetching spreadsheet\n[14:21:03] Candidate identified: John Doe\n[14:21:04] Reading candidate data\n[14:21:05] Analyzing Job Description\n[14:21:08] Gemini generation started\n[14:21:14] Resume JSON validated\n[14:21:15] Generating LaTeX\n[14:21:16] Compiling PDF\n[14:21:20] PDF compilation successful\n[14:21:21] Uploading to Google Drive\n[14:21:23] Drive upload successful\n[14:21:24] Updating Google Sheet\n[14:21:25] PIPELINE COMPLETED\nEach log entry should have:\ntimestamp\nstage\nseverity\nmessage\nSeverity:\nINFO\nSUCCESS\nWARNING\nERROR\nAllow:\nClear console\nCopy logs\nDownload logs if practical\n==================================================\n14. WORKSHEET ROW TRACKER\nCreate a table showing candidate processing status.\nColumns:\nRow\nCandidate\nTarget Role\nStatus\nGemini\nLaTeX\nPDF\nDrive\nSheets\nGenerated At\nResume\nUse status indicators.\nExample:\nJohn Doe | COMPLETED | \342\234\223 | \342\234\223 | \342\234\223 | \342\234\223 | \342\234\223\nAllow clicking a row to inspect the full pipeline execution.\n==================================================\n15. BULK PROCESSING\nSupport:\nPROCESS ONE\nPROCESS SELECTED\nPROCESS ALL\nFor bulk processing:\nProcess candidates sequentially by default.\nAvoid excessive API concurrency.\nRespect Gemini and Google API rate limits.\nDisplay progress.\nExample:\nProcessing 12 / 50\nDo not lose successful results if one candidate fails.\nA failed candidate should not stop the entire batch unless the user explicitly selects \"Stop on error.\"\n==================================================\n16. RETRY SYSTEM\nEvery pipeline stage should support retry where appropriate.\nFor example:\nRetry Gemini\nRetry LaTeX\nRetry PDF compilation\nRetry Drive upload\nRetry Sheets commit\nDo not regenerate the entire resume unnecessarily if only Drive upload failed.\nPersist enough pipeline state to resume from the failed stage.\n==================================================\n17. CONFIGURATION SETTINGS\nCreate a Settings panel containing:\nGOOGLE WORKSPACE\nGoogle account\nSpreadsheet\nWorksheet\nDrive folder\nColumn mapping\nAI\nGemini model\nGeneration temperature if supported\nMaximum retry count\nStrict factual mode\nRESUME\nTemplate\nPage target\nMaximum experience bullets\nMaximum projects\nFont\nMargins\nPIPELINE\nSequential/bulk mode\nStop on error\nRetry count\nLogging level\nSECURITY\nDrive sharing mode\nSession timeout\nCredential status\n==================================================\n18. CREDENTIALS UI\nCreate a secure credentials section.\nShow:\nGoogle Workspace OAuth\nConnected / Not Connected\nGemini\nConfigured / Not Configured\nNever display complete secrets.\nFor example:\nGemini API Key\n\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\342\200\242\nDo not store sensitive credentials in localStorage unless absolutely necessary.\nPrefer secure environment variables / platform secrets / OAuth token storage mechanisms supported by the deployment environment.\nIf the environment cannot securely store credentials, clearly explain what must be configured during deployment.\n==================================================\n19. ERROR HANDLING\nHandle:\nGoogle OAuth failure\nGoogle Sheets permission failure\nSpreadsheet not found\nWorksheet not found\nInvalid row\nMissing candidate information\nGemini API failure\nGemini rate limit\nInvalid Gemini response\nSchema validation failure\nLaTeX generation failure\nLaTeX compilation failure\nPDF generation failure\nDrive permission failure\nDrive upload failure\nSheets update failure\nNetwork failure\nEvery error should identify:\nStage\nCause\nSuggested action\nExample:\nDRIVE ERROR\nUnable to upload resume.\nPossible cause:\nThe authenticated Google account does not have permission to write to the selected Drive folder.\nAction:\nReconnect Google Workspace or select another Drive folder.\n==================================================\n20. UI DESIGN\nDesign a modern dark enterprise dashboard.\nVisual style:\ndark background\ncharcoal panels\nsubtle borders\nhigh contrast text\nrestrained accent color\nclean typography\ncompact information density\nrounded cards\nprofessional technical dashboard aesthetic\nTop section:\nResume Generation Pipeline\nv1.0.0\n[ RUN PIPELINE ]\nBelow:\nFETCH \342\206\222 SHEET \342\206\222 GEMINI \342\206\222 LATEX \342\206\222 COMPILE \342\206\222 DRIVE \342\206\222 COMMIT\nThen tabs:\nCONFIGURATION SETTINGS\nLIVE RUNTIME CONSOLE\nWORKSHEET ROW TRACKER\nGENERATED RESUMES\nDo not make the UI excessively flashy.\nPrioritize usability.\nThe application must be responsive for desktop and tablet.\n==================================================\n21. GENERATED RESUME PREVIEW\nAfter generating a resume, display:\nCandidate name\nTarget role\nATS keyword analysis\nGeneration status\nPDF preview if technically supported\nDownload PDF\nOpen Drive\nRegenerate\nView LaTeX\nView generation warnings\nAlso show:\nAI-generated content warnings\nFor example:\n\"3 fields were missing from the source data.\"\n==================================================\n22. SECURITY ARCHITECTURE\nTreat security as a first-class requirement.\nNever:\nhardcode Google OAuth secrets\nhardcode Gemini API keys\nexpose refresh tokens\nexpose service account private keys\nput credentials into client-side source\nmake Drive files public automatically\ntrust raw AI-generated LaTeX\nexecute arbitrary user-provided code\nUse:\nOAuth 2.0\nleast-privilege Google scopes\nenvironment secrets\ninput validation\noutput schema validation\nsanitized LaTeX\nauthenticated backend endpoints\nrate limiting where appropriate\n==================================================\n23. CODE ARCHITECTURE\nSeparate the application into logical modules.\nSuggested architecture:\n/src\n/components\n/pages\n/services\ngeminiService\nsheetsService\ndriveService\noauthService\nlatexService\npipelineService\n/types\n/schemas\n/utils\n/hooks\nBackend/API layer:\n/server\n/routes\n/services\n/auth\n/latex\n/google\n/gemini\nUse TypeScript if supported.\nCreate strong types for:\nCandidate\nJobDescription\nResume\nExperience\nProject\nPipelineState\nPipelineLog\nGoogleSheetConfig\nDriveConfig\nUse schema validation for external/API data.\n==================================================\n24. PIPELINE STATE MACHINE\nRepresent pipeline execution explicitly.\nExample:\nIDLE\n\342\206\223\nFETCHING\n\342\206\223\nSHEET_LOADED\n\342\206\223\nAI_GENERATING\n\342\206\223\nAI_VALIDATED\n\342\206\223\nLATEX_GENERATED\n\342\206\223\nPDF_COMPILING\n\342\206\223\nPDF_READY\n\342\206\223\nDRIVE_UPLOADING\n\342\206\223\nDRIVE_COMPLETE\n\342\206\223\nSHEETS_COMMITTING\n\342\206\223\nCOMPLETED\nAny stage may transition to:\nFAILED\nand expose:\nRetry\n==================================================\n25. DEMO MODE\nThe application must include a DEMO MODE so the UI can be tested without Google credentials.\nDemo mode should use realistic sample data.\nExample candidate:\nAlex Morgan\nB.Tech Computer Science\nPython\nJava\nSQL\nAWS\nReact\nMachine Learning\nCreate a sample Job Description.\nWhen Demo Mode is enabled:\nDo not call real Google APIs.\nDo not upload anything.\nSimulate pipeline stages.\nGenerate realistic logs.\nDemonstrate the complete UI.\nClearly label:\nDEMO MODE\nNever confuse simulated results with real results.\n==================================================\n26. IMPORTANT IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENT\nBefore implementing complicated features, inspect what Google AI Studio\'s current environment actually supports.\nDo not invent unavailable APIs or capabilities.\nIf something cannot run directly inside the AI Studio client:\nKeep the frontend interface.\nCreate a clean service abstraction.\nExplain the required backend/deployment component.\nProvide a working mock/demo implementation.\nKeep the production implementation replaceable.\nThe final application should therefore be architected so that:\nDEMO:\nBrowser\n\342\206\223\nMock Services\nPRODUCTION:\nBrowser\n\342\206\223\nSecure Backend/API\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Gemini\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Google OAuth\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Google Sheets API\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Google Drive API\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 LaTeX Compiler\n==================================================\n27. BUILD ORDER\nImplement in this order:\nPHASE 1\nCreate the complete dashboard UI and navigation.\nPHASE 2\nCreate the pipeline state machine and runtime console.\nPHASE 3\nCreate Demo Mode and make the entire pipeline work using mock services.\nPHASE 4\nImplement Gemini structured resume generation.\nPHASE 5\nImplement Google OAuth.\nPHASE 6\nImplement Google Sheets reading and writing.\nPHASE 7\nImplement LaTeX generation.\nPHASE 8\nImplement secure PDF compilation.\nPHASE 9\nImplement Google Drive upload.\nPHASE 10\nImplement Sheets commit.\nPHASE 11\nImplement bulk processing and retry/resume functionality.\nPHASE 12\nPerform security and error-handling review.\nDo not move to the next phase while the previous phase has broken functionality.\n==================================================\n28. FINAL SUCCESS CRITERIA\nThe finished application should allow this workflow:\nUser opens Resume Generation Pipeline.\nUser connects Google account.\nUser selects Google Spreadsheet.\nUser selects worksheet.\nUser maps columns.\nUser selects candidate.\nUser provides or selects Job Description.\nUser presses RUN PIPELINE.\nApplication fetches candidate data.\nApplication analyzes the Job Description.\nGemini generates structured resume content.\nApplication validates the Gemini response.\nApplication generates deterministic LaTeX.\nLaTeX is securely compiled to PDF.\nPDF is uploaded to Google Drive.\nDrive URL is obtained.\nGoogle Sheet is updated.\nUser sees COMPLETED.\nUser can preview/download/open the generated resume.\nAll stages and logs remain visible in the runtime console.\nBuild this as a real application, not merely a visual prototype.\nStart by creating the complete application architecture and functional Demo Mode, then progressively connect the real Google/Gemini services.\n"
    }
  }
}
